
// draft
// the iterables are finite lists of Int: the fragment has no generic element type and no infinite
// list
interleave(iterables: Int[][]) -> Int[]
	note "yield one element from each iterable in turn, round after round, until the shortest iterable is exhausted"
	# The output has as many elements as the number of iterables times the length of the shortest
	# iterable.
	all s in iterables: len(out) == len(iterables) * len(s) or (any t in iterables: len(t) < len(s))
	# For every position k below the length of the shortest iterable, the output's elements from
	# position k times the number of iterables up to but not including k plus one times the number
	# of iterables are the element at position k of each iterable, in the order the iterables are
	# given.
	// reaching for: a quantifier over integer positions, k from 0 up to but not including the
	// shortest iterable's length, and a way to build the list of each iterable's element at
	// position k in the order the iterables are given (indexing mapped over iterables), so I could
	// write: for every such k, out[k * len(iterables) .. (k + 1) * len(iterables)] == [it[k] for
	// each it in iterables]; the notation binds only list occurrences, never a bare position, and
	// has no index or map to form that list.
	// example wanted: no iterables at all; the length sentence has no shortest iterable to measure,
	// and the output is decided by what zero rounds mean
	// example wanted: [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5], [6, 7, 8]], the docstring case; the second iterable is
	// the shortest and decides how many rounds there are
	// example wanted: [[1, 2], [], [3]]; one iterable is empty, so there are zero rounds
	// example wanted: a single iterable [1, 2, 3]; each round has one element, so the rounds are
	// that iterable's positions
	// example wanted: [[1, 2], [3, 4]], equal lengths; every element of every iterable is reached
	// example wanted: [[5, 5], [5, 9]]; equal values in different iterables, so only position
	// decides which iterable each output element came from
